{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T170221Z-95fdafa1", "ts": "2026-10-27T17:04:57Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.1", "branch": "feature/1087-flaky-rerun"}, "body": {"base_commit": "0f6da645fe524dcb93c2c04c57dcc9efaee3a843", "base_tree": "ed6b50630692c703daa5f63f32860b299ee0f913", "head_tree": "b0999627aa5e6987e6817ad344021966a08a1ede", "head_commit": "c056f5f0369b8dcc1784c2f72eab36ed05053706", "dispatch_commit": "c056f5f0369b8dcc1784c2f72eab36ed05053706", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/pr_payload.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_finding_scope_rule.py", "tests/test_pr_review_payload.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md", "plugin/lib/briefing.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/pr_payload.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_finding_scope_rule.py", "tests/test_pr_review_payload.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Fallback --rerun-failed retries under sys.executable, not the interpreter the run used (semantic conflict with the merged #1097 python floor)", "recommendation": "In _rerun_failures the fallback argv is `[sys.executable, \"-m\", \"pytest\", ...]`, but since the develop sync in c056f5f0 the run itself uses `_fallback_pytest_python()`, which returns the interpreter the caller invoked (REEXECUTED_FROM) rather than the one the Python floor re-executed the hook into. When the floor re-execs, the retry runs under a different interpreter than the run did. If that interpreter lacks the project's packages or pytest, the retry fails closed and the feature does nothing. Worse, a test that fails for a real reason under the project interpreter (version- or environment-dependent) can pass under the hook's interpreter and get recorded as a flake, which turns the record green. That is exactly the hidden failure the retry must not produce. Use `_fallback_pytest_python()` in the rerun argv, and add a test that pins the rerun to the same interpreter as the run (monkeypatch `_python_floor.REEXECUTED_FROM`). The merged-in branch never saw this helper, which is why no current test catches it.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_test_evidence_flaky.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "Touched judgment compares a cwd-relative file list against a repo-root-relative diff", "recommendation": "_judge_flakes resolves the flake's file against `git ls-files` run in project_dir (paths relative to that directory), then checks it against `coverage._coverage_changed_files`, whose `git diff --name-only` paths are relative to the repo top level. When project_dir is the repo root (the normal case) they agree. If a governed project lives in a subdirectory of its git repo, a changed test file reads as `touched: false`, the lenient answer, and `block` would credit it. Either confirm that a subdirectory project_dir is unsupported, or normalise both sides (e.g. `git diff --relative`, or ls-files with `--full-name`). The coverage verifier shares this base, so the mismatch predates this branch; only the use for an authority decision is new.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "severity": "note", "title": "State template lost its test_commands list-entry example", "recommendation": "The trim of plugin/templates/project-state.yaml deleted `#   - npm run test:ci -- --junit --outputFile={junit_xml}` under `# test_commands:`, so the template no longer shows that key's block-list shape, the same shape `test_rerun_commands:` must now match by position. Restore one example entry, or confirm the docs reference is enough.", "files": ["plugin/templates/project-state.yaml"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The flaky_policy Critic rule and the PR body instruction match delegate-run flaky: lines from sibling worktrees, which were judged against a different branch's diff", "recommendation": "Class: every reader keyed on the printed `flaky:` text. `cmd_test_status` now prints the saved run's flakes (`flaky: ... [touched by this branch; flaky_policy: warn]`). It also appends `_narrowed_run_lines`, which now emits `    flaky: ...` lines for the newest narrowed run of every scratch worktree in the clone-shared store. Nothing filters those by branch or age, and each was judged by `_judge_flakes` against that worktree's own base and diff. The new Goal 1 clause in goals-1-3.md and review-protocol.md ('A `flaky:` line tagged `flaky_policy: warn` -> WARNING') and the PR SKILL Step 5 sentence ('Every `flaky:` line `test-status` prints goes under a Passed only on retry heading: one tagged touched or unknown is the branch's to explain') both match the indented delegate lines too. So a reviewer warns, and a PR body reports 'touched by this branch', for a flake a sibling branch's delegate recorded. The PR reviewer payload (`pr_payload._section_test_evidence`) carries only `saved_flaky_lines`, so the PR skill's 'every line test-status prints' and the payload the PR reviewer reads already disagree about the set. Why it broke: the rule is keyed on a text two producers emit with different subjects. Fix it with one construction rather than prose: give delegate flakes a prefix or tag the rule cannot match (for example, drop the policy tag from narrowed-run flake lines, or print them as `delegate flaky:`). Or scope both instructions to the saved run's lines and pin that with a test that renders a narrowed-run fact carrying a `warn` flake and asserts the rule's matched text does not appear.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "_flake_test_file builds a synthetic <testcase> XML element to reuse _pytest_node_id's classname resolver", "recommendation": "Instance. `_flake_test_file` splits the flake id back into classname and name, then builds `ET.Element(\"testcase\", {...})` only so it can call `_pytest_node_id`, whose real input is (classname, name, file). The touched judgment now depends on the node-id builder's XML-element signature, and the classname-to-file walk exists once in pytest form and again in a hand-rolled any-language form a few lines below. If this code is touched again, extract a `(classname, name, file_attr) -> (node_id, test_file)` helper that both callers use, and let `_pytest_node_id` wrap it. No change is needed now.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "State template's commented test_commands: lost its example item", "recommendation": "Instance, prose. The trim removed `#   - npm run test:ci -- --junit --outputFile={junit_xml}`. That leaves `# test_commands:    (several environments; instead of test_command)` as a bare block key. If someone uncomments it as written, they get an empty block, which the reader refuses as unparseable. The refusal message shows the right shape, so nobody is silently misled. Either restore one example item or leave it as is. The full syntax lives in project-state-keys.md.", "files": ["plugin/templates/project-state.yaml"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "_judge_flakes swallows a failed `git ls-files`, and each flake's touched_why then names the wrong cause", "recommendation": "Instance. In plugin/bin/prawduct-hook `_judge_flakes`, a nonzero `run_git(ls-files ...)` leaves `files` empty with no message. Every flake that `_flake_test_file` resolves through `files` (a report `file` attribute, or the non-pytest classname path) then gets touched=null with touched_why \"the report's file X matches no file here\" or \"no file here matches <class>\". The real cause is that the file list could not be read. The fallback direction is correct (null is read as touched, the strict answer). Only the stated reason is wrong, and under `flaky_policy: block` that is the text an operator debugs from. This is the gap hook-surface's rule describes: \"Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: ... degraded advice names its consequence, and a swallow is fixed in the frame that discards it\". Fix it in that frame: when rc != 0, set a files_why (\"the repo's file list could not be read (<stderr>)\"), use it as touched_why the way diff_why is used, and print it on stderr once like the diff note. It is rare, because the diff read through git just succeeded, so NOTE.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "A rerun that never ran also reports 'still failing after N retries'", "recommendation": "Instance. In `_rerun_failures`, an OSError at launch or an unreadable rerun report `break`s out of the attempt loop with `pending` still full. The `if pending:` branch then appends \"still failing after {attempts_max} retr(ies): ...\", even though no retry produced a result. The earlier note does name the launch or parse failure, so a reader can work it out, but the second line claims the tests failed again. Gate the 'still failing' note on at least one rerun report having been read (e.g. track a `ran` flag), so an infrastructure failure does not read as the test's own red.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope none. Re-raised because the plan's decision list changed materially after R-14 (commits 11d31af5 and 5627f675 added the boundary-review corrections). The owner settled three forks in the alignment pass (per-command rerun template, `warn` default, one chunked branch). The builder settled these mid-build: (1) a passing retry clears a command only when its own exit was 1 ('authority fails closed'; corrected at the boundary); (2) `block` returns 1 directly instead of inventing a per-command status ('do it right'); a restamp is not re-judged; (3) the untouched tag reads 'outside this branch's diff', and the policy's answer is stored as `outcome` at record time, so the Critic rule keys on `flaky_policy: warn` alone; (4) the flake's file is resolved repo-relative or reported unknown, never guessed lenient; (5) an 18-token reviewer-payload raise, priced against NFR 'Review wall-clock'; (6) Chunk 02's product verification on this repo was replaced by the real-pytest tests, because a `test_rerun_commands:` declaration here is the owner's call. The tradeoff that departs from the owner's option wording (no built-in rerun for declared commands) is recorded with its reason. The five 'Inferences (flagged for the boundary review)' are still builder inferences the owner has not confirmed: retry ceiling 5 and failure threshold 5, the `{pytest_node_ids}`/`{junit_xml}` placeholders, positional `test_rerun_commands`, the 300-character `first_failure`, and refusal with ingest/restamp. Each is a module constant or an additive key and cheap to change, so they are listed here for the owner rather than rated. The architecture departure (the recorder reruns product tooling) has a vetoable [DECISION] block. No alignment-pass miss found.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings cross-check, backlog reconciliation and Records Pass coverage", "recommendation": "Scope none. Learnings: read core.md plus the area files `learnings-files --for-diff` printed (authoring, gates, hook-surface, pr, release, reviews, tests). No change reintroduces a warned-against pattern. core's 'A channel that is produced and never consumed is a DEFECT' holds: `touched_why`, `outcome` and `source` are printed by `gates._flake_judgment_tag`/`flaky_lines`, and the narrowed fact's `flaky` is read by `_narrowed_run_lines`. The one hook-surface citation folds into the ls-files finding above. No learnings files changed, so the added-rule pass is empty. Backlog: `affecting` on the changed code returns #672 and #928, neither of which this resolves. The search for flaky/rerun/test-evidence finds nothing newly resolved besides #1087, already dispositioned as R-15 (priors). The deferred item #1088 exists and is open (`resolve` returns via=id), so the plan's 'file the deferred covered-code backlog item' is met. #281 is already closed. Records Pass over files_oracle (.prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md, .prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md): checked the fragment's Chunk 01\u201304 claims and Consumer note, and the plan's Architecture, Inferences, Persisted-format and decision claims, against the code. Checked: refusal with --from-junit/--from-counts/--no-rerun via `_sources`; skipped-on-retry stays red; the block exit returns 1; restamp carries flakes; the handoff 'Flaky Tests' section; delegate-run flake lines. No claim clears a bar (ships false, misleads into action, or must stop the merge). priors: R-10, R-14, R-15 and R-16 acknowledged; R-14 is re-listed above only because the plan's decision list changed after it.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md"], "fid": "R-10"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 2, "note": 8}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "flaky-rerun", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunk": null, "base_reviewed": "0f6da645fe524dcb93c2c04c57dcc9efaee3a843", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-flaky-rerun.md", ".prawduct/changes/flaky-rerun.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/docs/test-report-contract.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/pr/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0}}, "dispatched_at": "2026-10-27T17:02:22Z"}}

